<div class="section">
  <div class="container intro-split">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 style="margin-top:0">What is a TRA?</h2>
      <p>A tenants' and residents' association is a group of people who live in the same area and work together to improve it. Members might be council tenants, housing association tenants, leaseholders or homeowners.</p>
      <p>Groups raise issues with landlords and the council, take part in consultation, and often organise activities that bring neighbours together.</p>
      <p>You don't need any experience to start one. You need a few neighbours who care about where they live.</p>
    </div>
    <figure class="figure">
      {% picture "6815366", "(min-width: 60em) 40vw, 100vw" %}
      <figcaption>{% credit "6815366" %}</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</div>

<section class="section section--stone" id="starting" aria-labelledby="starting-title">
  <div class="container grid">
    <div class="span-4">
      <h2 id="starting-title">Starting a group</h2>
      <p class="mt-s muted">We'll support you at every step. Here's roughly how it works.</p>
    </div>
    <ol class="steps span-8">
      <li><div><h3>Get in touch with us</h3><p>Tell us where you live and what's happening in your area. We'll talk through whether a group is the right next step.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><h3>Find other residents</h3><p>We'll help you reach neighbours and plan a first meeting in a local venue.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><h3>Agree how your group will work</h3><p>We'll help you write a constitution, elect a committee and open a bank account.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><h3>Get recognised and get heard</h3><p>We'll help you build a working relationship with your landlord and the council, and keep supporting you as the group grows.</p></div></li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="running" aria-labelledby="running-title">
  <div class="container grid">
    <div class="span-4">
      <h2 id="running-title">Running your group</h2>
      <p class="mt-s muted">Common questions from committees. Ask us about anything that isn't covered here.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="accordion span-8">
      <details>
        <summary>Do we need a constitution?</summary>
        <div class="accordion__body"><p>Yes. A constitution sets out what your group is for, who can join, how decisions are made and how money is handled. Landlords and funders usually ask to see one. We can help you write yours or check an existing one.</p></div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What happens at an AGM?</summary>
        <div class="accordion__body"><p>At your annual general meeting, members hear what the group has done over the year, see the accounts and elect the committee. We can help you plan it and make sure it follows your constitution.</p></div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>How should we manage the group's money?</summary>
        <div class="accordion__body"><p>Keep the group's money in a bank account in its name, with at least two people needed to approve payments. Keep simple records of money in and out, and share them with members. We can talk you through setting this up.</p></div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Where can we find funding?</summary>
        <div class="accordion__body"><p>Small grants are often available for community activities and running costs. Tell us what you'd like to do and we can help you look for suitable funding and put an application together.</p></div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>How do we work with our landlord or the council?</summary>
        <div class="accordion__body"><p>Groups get the best results when they have a regular, constructive relationship with the people who make decisions. We can help you set up meetings, prepare what you want to say and follow up afterwards.</p></div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Our group has stopped meeting. Can you help?</summary>
        <div class="accordion__body"><p>Yes. Groups go quiet for all sorts of reasons. We can help you bring people back together, refresh your committee and decide what to focus on next.</p></div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

